Can a reader upgrade to a writer? <p>Not safely. Two readers trying to upgrade each wait for the other to leave, and that's a deadlock. <code>ReentrantReadWriteLock</code> forbids upgrades but allows downgrading (acquire read while holding write, then release write).</p> Make it reentrant. <p>Track the owner thread and a hold count for the writer, and a per-thread read count (a <code>ThreadLocal</code> or a map) for readers.</p> When is a RW lock slower than a plain lock? <p>When critical sections are tiny: the bookkeeping costs more than the parallelism gains. Consider <code>StampedLock</code> optimistic reads, or immutable snapshots in an <code>AtomicReference</code>.</p> Full source: SimpleReadWriteLock.java <pre><code>import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

/**
 * Implement a read-write lock: many concurrent readers OR one writer. Writer-preferring to avoid writer starvation.
 * Run: java SimpleReadWriteLock.java
 */
public class SimpleReadWriteLock {

    private int activeReaders;          // readers currently holding the lock
    private int waitingWriters;         // writers queued: new readers must back off (writer preference)
    private boolean writerActive;       // a writer holds the lock

    public synchronized void lockRead() throws InterruptedException {
        while (writerActive || waitingWriters &gt; 0) {
            wait();
        }
        activeReaders++;
    }

    public synchronized void unlockRead() {
        if (activeReaders &lt;= 0) throw new IllegalMonitorStateException("no read lock held");
        activeReaders--;
        if (activeReaders == 0) notifyAll();          // last reader out lets a writer in
    }

    public synchronized void lockWrite() throws InterruptedException {
        waitingWriters++;
        try {
            while (writerActive || activeReaders &gt; 0) {
                wait();
            }
        } finally {
            waitingWriters--;                          // also on interrupt, or readers would block forever
        }
        writerActive = true;
    }

    public synchronized void unlockWrite() {
        if (!writerActive) throw new IllegalMonitorStateException("no write lock held");
        writerActive = false;
        notifyAll();                                   // wake both readers and writers
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        var rw = new SimpleReadWriteLock();
        AtomicInteger readersInside = new AtomicInteger(), writersInside = new AtomicInteger();
        AtomicInteger maxReaders = new AtomicInteger(), violations = new AtomicInteger();
        long[] counter = {0};                          // shared data protected by rw

        // Platform threads on purpose: this lock is built on Object.wait(). On JDK 21-23 a virtual thread in
        // Object.wait() PINS its carrier; 2,000 waiting VTs exhaust the carrier pool (max 256) and the program
        // hangs. JDK 24+ (JEP 491) fixes that; on 21 use a Lock/Condition-based design with VTs instead.
        try (var ex = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(64)) {
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; 2_000; i++) {
                boolean writer = i % 10 == 0;
                ex.submit(() -&gt; {
                    if (writer) {
                        rw.lockWrite();
                        try {
                            if (writersInside.incrementAndGet() != 1 || readersInside.get() != 0) violations.incrementAndGet();
                            counter[0]++;
                            Thread.sleep(1);
                            writersInside.decrementAndGet();
                        } finally { rw.unlockWrite(); }
                    } else {
                        rw.lockRead();
                        try {
                            int now = readersInside.incrementAndGet();
                            maxReaders.accumulateAndGet(now, Math::max);
                            if (writersInside.get() != 0) violations.incrementAndGet();
                            Thread.sleep(1);
                            readersInside.decrementAndGet();
                        } finally { rw.unlockRead(); }
                    }
                    return null;
                });
            }
        }
        System.out.printf("writes=%d violations=%d maxConcurrentReaders=%d%n", counter[0], violations.get(), maxReaders.get());
        if (violations.get() != 0 || counter[0] != 200 || maxReaders.get() &lt; 2) throw new AssertionError();
        System.out.println("PASS");
    }
}
</code></pre>

How does <code>ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor</code> / <code>DelayQueue</code> do it? <p>The same way: a heap plus a <code>Condition</code> with <code>awaitNanos</code>. They also add a leader thread (the Leader-Follower pattern): only one thread waits timed for the head, while the others wait indefinitely. That avoids many threads all waking at the same deadline.</p> Add fixed-rate repetition. <p>After running, re-insert the job with <code>due += period</code>. Use the previous due time, not <code>now</code>, to avoid drift. Catch exceptions so one failure doesn't silently cancel future runs.</p> What about millions of timers? <p>A heap costs O(log n) per operation. Hashed or hierarchical timing wheels (Netty <code>HashedWheelTimer</code>, Kafka) give O(1) inserts at some cost in precision.</p> Durability? <p>In-memory timers die with the process. Use DB-backed schedulers (Quartz, <code>SKIP LOCKED</code> polling) for anything that must survive restarts.</p> Full source: DelayedTaskScheduler.java <pre><code>import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.Condition;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

/**
 * Build a mini ScheduledExecutorService: schedule(task, delay). One dispatcher thread sleeps exactly until the
 * earliest task is due, and wakes early if an even earlier task is scheduled. Due tasks run on virtual threads.
 * Run: java DelayedTaskScheduler.java
 */
public class DelayedTaskScheduler implements AutoCloseable {

    private record Job(long dueNanos, long seq, Runnable task) implements Comparable&lt;Job&gt; {
        public int compareTo(Job o) {
            int c = Long.compare(dueNanos, o.dueNanos);
            return c != 0 ? c : Long.compare(seq, o.seq);      // FIFO for equal due times
        }
    }

    private final PriorityQueue&lt;Job&gt; queue = new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;();   // min-heap by due time, guarded by lock
    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
    private final Condition changed = lock.newCondition();           // "new head or shutdown"
    private final AtomicLong seq = new AtomicLong();
    private final Thread dispatcher;
    private boolean shutdown;                                        // guarded by lock

    public DelayedTaskScheduler() {
        dispatcher = Thread.ofPlatform().name("scheduler-dispatcher").daemon(true).start(this::dispatchLoop);
    }

    public void schedule(Runnable task, long delay, TimeUnit unit) {
        Job job = new Job(System.nanoTime() + unit.toNanos(delay), seq.getAndIncrement(), task);
        lock.lock();
        try {
            if (shutdown) throw new IllegalStateException("scheduler is shut down");
            queue.add(job);
            if (queue.peek() == job) changed.signal();   // new earliest job → dispatcher must re-compute its sleep
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    private void dispatchLoop() {
        while (true) {
            Job due;
            lock.lock();
            try {
                while (true) {
                    if (shutdown) return;
                    Job head = queue.peek();
                    if (head == null) {
                        changed.await();                              // nothing scheduled: sleep until schedule()
                        continue;
                    }
                    long wait = head.dueNanos() - System.nanoTime();
                    if (wait &lt;= 0) {
                        due = queue.poll();                           // it's time
                        break;
                    }
                    changed.awaitNanos(wait);                         // sleep until due, or until a new head arrives
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                return;
            } finally {
                lock.unlock();
            }
            Thread.ofVirtual().start(due.task());                     // never run user code on the dispatcher
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void close() {
        lock.lock();
        try { shutdown = true; changed.signal(); } finally { lock.unlock(); }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        List&lt;String&gt; order = Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList&lt;&gt;());
        CountDownLatch done = new CountDownLatch(5);
        long t0 = System.nanoTime();
        try (var s = new DelayedTaskScheduler()) {
            s.schedule(() -&gt; { order.add("C@300"); done.countDown(); }, 300, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
            s.schedule(() -&gt; { order.add("A@100"); done.countDown(); }, 100, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
            s.schedule(() -&gt; { order.add("E@500"); done.countDown(); }, 500, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
            Thread.sleep(50);
            s.schedule(() -&gt; { order.add("B@150"); done.countDown(); }, 100, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS); // new head mid-sleep
            s.schedule(() -&gt; { order.add("D@450"); done.countDown(); }, 400, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
            if (!done.await(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) throw new AssertionError("tasks did not run");
        }
        long ms = (System.nanoTime() - t0) / 1_000_000;
        System.out.println("execution order: " + order + " finished after " + ms + " ms");
        if (!order.equals(List.of("A@100", "B@150", "C@300", "D@450", "E@500"))) throw new AssertionError();
        System.out.println("PASS");
    }
}
</code></pre>

Ordering vs tryLock: which do you prefer? <p>Ordering: it's simple, it has no retries, and it's deterministic, but it requires knowing every lock up front. <code>tryLock</code> handles dynamic lock sets and gives timeouts, but costs retries and the risk of livelock. Default to ordering.</p> Can a single thread deadlock itself? <p>With non-reentrant locks, yes: re-acquiring a <code>StampedLock</code> or a <code>Semaphore(1)</code>. It also happens when a thread waits for a task it submitted to a full single-thread pool (pool-induced deadlock).</p> Can virtual threads deadlock? <p>Yes, with the same lock cycles. On JDK 21–23 there's an extra form, carrier starvation: all carriers pinned by VTs waiting on a monitor held by an unmounted VT. JEP 491 (JDK 24) fixes that.</p> Full source: DeadlockDetectAndFix.java <pre><code>import java.lang.management.ManagementFactory;
import java.lang.management.ThreadInfo;
import java.lang.management.ThreadMXBean;
import java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

/**
 * Create a deadlock on purpose, detect it with ThreadMXBean, then show two fixes (lock ordering and tryLock).
 * Run: java DeadlockDetectAndFix.java
 */
public class DeadlockDetectAndFix {

    static void createDeadlock(Object a, Object b) {
        CountDownLatch bothHoldFirstLock = new CountDownLatch(2);   // guarantees the bad interleaving
        Thread.ofPlatform().name("worker-A").daemon(true).start(() -&gt; {
            synchronized (a) {
                bothHoldFirstLock.countDown();
                await(bothHoldFirstLock);
                synchronized (b) { System.out.println("never printed"); }
            }
        });
        Thread.ofPlatform().name("worker-B").daemon(true).start(() -&gt; {
            synchronized (b) {                                      // opposite order → cycle
                bothHoldFirstLock.countDown();
                await(bothHoldFirstLock);
                synchronized (a) { System.out.println("never printed"); }
            }
        });
    }

    static long[] detect() throws InterruptedException {
        ThreadMXBean mx = ManagementFactory.getThreadMXBean();
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 50; i++) {                              // poll up to ~5 s
            long[] ids = mx.findDeadlockedThreads();                // monitors AND j.u.c locks
            if (ids != null) {
                for (ThreadInfo info : mx.getThreadInfo(ids, true, true)) {
                    System.out.printf("  DEADLOCK: %s is waiting for %s held by %s%n",
                            info.getThreadName(), info.getLockName(), info.getLockOwnerName());
                }
                return ids;
            }
            Thread.sleep(100);
        }
        return null;
    }

    /** Fix 1: every thread acquires locks in the same global order (here: by id). No cycle is possible. */
    record Resource(int id, ReentrantLock lock) {}

    static void withBoth(Resource x, Resource y, Runnable action) {
        Resource first = x.id() &lt; y.id() ? x : y;
        Resource second = first == x ? y : x;
        first.lock().lock();
        try {
            second.lock().lock();
            try { action.run(); } finally { second.lock().unlock(); }
        } finally {
            first.lock().unlock();
        }
    }

    /** Fix 2: never wait for the second lock while holding the first: tryLock with timeout, back off, retry. */
    static boolean withBothTryLock(ReentrantLock x, ReentrantLock y, Runnable action) throws InterruptedException {
        long deadline = System.nanoTime() + TimeUnit.SECONDS.toNanos(5);
        while (System.nanoTime() &lt; deadline) {
            if (x.tryLock(10, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
                try {
                    if (y.tryLock(10, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
                        try { action.run(); return true; } finally { y.unlock(); }
                    }
                } finally {
                    x.unlock();                                     // release what we hold → breaks hold-and-wait
                }
            }
            Thread.sleep(java.util.concurrent.ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextInt(1, 5)); // random backoff vs livelock
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        System.out.println("1) creating a deadlock...");
        createDeadlock(new Object(), new Object());
        long[] ids = detect();
        if (ids == null || ids.length != 2) throw new AssertionError("deadlock not detected");

        System.out.println("2) lock ordering: 2 threads x 100k opposite-order acquisitions");
        Resource r1 = new Resource(1, new ReentrantLock()), r2 = new Resource(2, new ReentrantLock());
        int[] counter = {0};
        Thread t1 = Thread.ofVirtual().start(() -&gt; { for (int i = 0; i &lt; 100_000; i++) withBoth(r1, r2, () -&gt; counter[0]++); });
        Thread t2 = Thread.ofVirtual().start(() -&gt; { for (int i = 0; i &lt; 100_000; i++) withBoth(r2, r1, () -&gt; counter[0]++); });
        if (!t1.join(java.time.Duration.ofSeconds(20)) || !t2.join(java.time.Duration.ofSeconds(20))) throw new AssertionError("stuck");
        System.out.println("  completed, counter=" + counter[0]);

        System.out.println("3) tryLock + backoff: 2 threads x 10k opposite-order acquisitions");
        ReentrantLock a = new ReentrantLock(), b = new ReentrantLock();
        int[] c2 = {0};
        Thread u1 = Thread.ofVirtual().start(() -&gt; uncheck(() -&gt; { for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10_000; i++) if (!withBothTryLock(a, b, () -&gt; c2[0]++)) throw new AssertionError(); }));
        Thread u2 = Thread.ofVirtual().start(() -&gt; uncheck(() -&gt; { for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10_000; i++) if (!withBothTryLock(b, a, () -&gt; c2[0]++)) throw new AssertionError(); }));
        u1.join(); u2.join();
        System.out.println("  completed, counter=" + c2[0]);
        if (counter[0] != 200_000 || c2[0] != 20_000) throw new AssertionError();
        System.out.println("PASS");                     // the two deadlocked daemon threads die with the JVM
    }

    static void await(CountDownLatch l) {
        try { l.await(); } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
    }

    interface Body { void run() throws InterruptedException; }

    static void uncheck(Body b) {
        try { b.run(); } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
    }
}
</code></pre>

Resource ordering Waiter (N-1 seats) <code>tryLock</code> both + back-off Deadlock-free ✔ ✔ ✔ Starvation-free With fair locks With a fair semaphore Probabilistically Extra coordination None A global semaphore (a contention point) Retries General lesson Global lock order Limit concurrency Don't hold and wait What's the real-world version of this problem? <p>Any operation that needs several locks at once: transferring between two accounts (problem 14), moving items between two inventory bins, or updating two DB rows (keep a consistent row-locking order).</p> Full source: DiningPhilosophers.java <pre><code>import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.Semaphore;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicIntegerArray;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

/**
 * LeetCode 1226. N philosophers, N forks, each needs both neighbouring forks to eat. Avoid deadlock and starvation.
 * Run: java DiningPhilosophers.java
 */
public class DiningPhilosophers {

    /** Solution 1: resource ordering. Everyone picks up the LOWER-numbered fork first, so no wait-for cycle exists. */
    static final class OrderedTable {
        private final ReentrantLock[] forks;

        OrderedTable(int n) {
            forks = new ReentrantLock[n];
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) forks[i] = new ReentrantLock(true);   // fair → no starvation
        }

        void dine(int philosopher, Runnable eat) {
            int left = philosopher;
            int right = (philosopher + 1) % forks.length;
            ReentrantLock first = forks[Math.min(left, right)];
            ReentrantLock second = forks[Math.max(left, right)];
            first.lock();
            try {
                second.lock();
                try { eat.run(); } finally { second.unlock(); }
            } finally {
                first.unlock();
            }
        }
    }

    /** Solution 2: a "waiter" admits at most N-1 philosophers to the table, so at least one can always get both forks. */
    static final class WaiterTable {
        private final ReentrantLock[] forks;
        private final Semaphore seats;

        WaiterTable(int n) {
            forks = new ReentrantLock[n];
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) forks[i] = new ReentrantLock();
            seats = new Semaphore(n - 1, true);
        }

        void dine(int philosopher, Runnable eat) throws InterruptedException {
            seats.acquire();
            try {
                ReentrantLock left = forks[philosopher], right = forks[(philosopher + 1) % forks.length];
                left.lock();                      // naive left-then-right is now safe
                try {
                    right.lock();
                    try { eat.run(); } finally { right.unlock(); }
                } finally { left.unlock(); }
            } finally {
                seats.release();
            }
        }
    }

    interface Table { void dine(int p, Runnable eat) throws InterruptedException; }

    static void simulate(String name, Table table, int n, int meals) throws Exception {
        AtomicIntegerArray eaten = new AtomicIntegerArray(n);
        List&lt;Thread&gt; ts = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
        for (int p = 0; p &lt; n; p++) {
            int id = p;
            ts.add(Thread.ofVirtual().start(() -&gt; {
                try {
                    for (int m = 0; m &lt; meals; m++) table.dine(id, () -&gt; eaten.incrementAndGet(id));
                } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
            }));
        }
        for (Thread t : ts) if (!t.join(Duration.ofSeconds(30))) throw new AssertionError(name + ": deadlock!");
        for (int p = 0; p &lt; n; p++) if (eaten.get(p) != meals) throw new AssertionError(name + " p" + p);
        System.out.printf("%-15s %d philosophers x %,d meals each: all finished, no deadlock%n", name, n, meals);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        var ordered = new OrderedTable(5);
        simulate("resource-order", ordered::dine, 5, 20_000);
        var waiter = new WaiterTable(5);
        simulate("waiter", waiter::dine, 5, 20_000);
        System.out.println("PASS");
    }
}
</code></pre>

The audit blocks all transfers. Can you avoid that? <p>Yes, with MVCC or snapshots: keep an append-only ledger (double-entry: every transfer writes −amount and +amount entries in one atomic step), then compute balances as of a ledger position. That's how real wallets and DBs work.</p> What about across many service instances? <p>In-JVM locks don't help there. Use one DB transaction with <code>SELECT … FOR UPDATE</code> on both rows in id order, or optimistic <code>@Version</code> with retries, plus an idempotency key per transfer. See Distributed Concurrency.</p> Could you use <code>synchronized</code> instead of ReentrantLock? <p>Yes: <code>synchronized (first) { synchronized (second) { … } }</code>. On JDK 21–23 that's fine because nothing blocks inside (pure memory updates). <code>ReentrantLock</code> adds <code>tryLock</code> if you want timeouts.</p> Full source: BankTransfer.java <pre><code>import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.ThreadLocalRandom;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

/**
 * Thread-safe money transfer between accounts: no lost updates, no deadlock, total money conserved.
 * Money is held in minor units (paise) as long — never double.
 * Run: java BankTransfer.java
 */
public class BankTransfer {

    static final class Account {
        private final long id;
        private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
        private long balanceMinor;                       // guarded by lock

        Account(long id, long openingMinor) { this.id = id; this.balanceMinor = openingMinor; }

        long balance() {
            lock.lock();
            try { return balanceMinor; } finally { lock.unlock(); }
        }
    }

    static final AtomicLong rejected = new AtomicLong();

    /** @return true if moved, false if insufficient funds */
    static boolean transfer(Account from, Account to, long amountMinor) {
        if (amountMinor &lt;= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("amount must be positive");
        if (from == to) return true;                     // same lock twice would be fine (reentrant) but pointless

        Account first = from.id &lt; to.id ? from : to;     // global lock order by id → no deadlock cycle
        Account second = first == from ? to : from;

        first.lock.lock();
        try {
            second.lock.lock();
            try {
                if (from.balanceMinor &lt; amountMinor) {   // check-then-act is SAFE: both locks are held
                    rejected.incrementAndGet();
                    return false;
                }
                from.balanceMinor -= amountMinor;
                to.balanceMinor += amountMinor;
                return true;
            } finally {
                second.lock.unlock();
            }
        } finally {
            first.lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    /** Consistent snapshot of the total: lock ALL accounts in id order, then sum. */
    static long auditTotal(Account[] accounts) {
        for (Account a : accounts) a.lock.lock();        // accounts[] is sorted by id
        try {
            long total = 0;
            for (Account a : accounts) total += a.balanceMinor;
            return total;
        } finally {
            for (int i = accounts.length - 1; i &gt;= 0; i--) accounts[i].lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        int n = 50;
        Account[] accounts = new Account[n];
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) accounts[i] = new Account(i, 1_000_00);    // ₹1,000.00 each
        long before = auditTotal(accounts);

        long[] midAudit = new long[1];
        try (var ex = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
            for (int t = 0; t &lt; 200_000; t++) {
                ex.submit(() -&gt; {
                    var rnd = ThreadLocalRandom.current();
                    transfer(accounts[rnd.nextInt(n)], accounts[rnd.nextInt(n)], rnd.nextLong(1, 50_00));
                });
                if (t == 100_000) ex.submit(() -&gt; midAudit[0] = auditTotal(accounts));
            }
        }
        long after = auditTotal(accounts);
        System.out.printf("200,000 concurrent transfers: total before=%d after=%d mid-run audit=%d, rejected(insufficient)=%d%n",
                before, after, midAudit[0], rejected.get());
        if (before != after || midAudit[0] != before) throw new AssertionError("money created or destroyed!");
        for (Account a : accounts) if (a.balance() &lt; 0) throw new AssertionError("negative balance");
        System.out.println("PASS");
    }
}
