How would you cancel the pipeline on error? <p>Have any stage that fails call <code>executor.shutdownNow()</code> (which interrupts all stages), or use a <code>StructuredTaskScope</code> (fail-fast, cancels the siblings). Consumers should treat <code>InterruptedException</code> as "stop".</p> How do you preserve input order with N parallel parsers? <p>Tag each line with a sequence number, and have the aggregator reorder with a small buffer (a <code>PriorityQueue</code> keyed by sequence). Alternatively, partition by key so that ordering only matters per key.</p> Is this better than parallel streams? <p>For IO-bound stages (DB lookups, HTTP), yes: virtual threads handle blocking cheaply and the queues bound memory. For pure CPU transforms on an in-memory collection, <code>parallelStream()</code> is simpler.</p> Full source: ProducerConsumerPoisonPill.java <pre><code>import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.ArrayBlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;

/**
 * Multi-stage pipeline: reader → parser workers → aggregator, connected by bounded queues and shut down with
 * poison pills. Uses virtual threads, so blocking put/take is cheap.
 * Run: java ProducerConsumerPoisonPill.java
 */
public class ProducerConsumerPoisonPill {

    sealed interface Msg permits Line, Poison {}
    record Line(String text) implements Msg {}
    record Poison() implements Msg {}                       // "no more data" marker
    static final Poison POISON = new Poison();

    static long run(List&lt;String&gt; input, int parsers) throws Exception {
        BlockingQueue&lt;Msg&gt; lines = new ArrayBlockingQueue&lt;&gt;(100);     // stage 1 → 2 (bounded = backpressure)
        BlockingQueue&lt;Long&gt; numbers = new ArrayBlockingQueue&lt;&gt;(100);  // stage 2 → 3
        final long END = Long.MIN_VALUE;                               // poison for stage 3
        AtomicLong total = new AtomicLong();

        try (var ex = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
            // Stage 1: single producer
            ex.submit(() -&gt; {
                for (String s : input) lines.put(new Line(s));
                for (int i = 0; i &lt; parsers; i++) lines.put(POISON);   // ONE pill per consumer
                return null;
            });

            // Stage 2: N parallel parsers
            AtomicInteger finishedParsers = new AtomicInteger();
            for (int p = 0; p &lt; parsers; p++) {
                ex.submit(() -&gt; {
                    while (true) {
                        Msg m = lines.take();
                        if (m instanceof Poison) break;                 // swallow my pill and stop
                        if (m instanceof Line(String text)) numbers.put(Long.parseLong(text.trim()));
                    }
                    if (finishedParsers.incrementAndGet() == parsers) {    // exactly ONE parser sees this
                        numbers.put(END);                                   // last parser out poisons stage 3
                    }
                    return null;
                });
            }

            // Stage 3: single aggregator
            ex.submit(() -&gt; {
                long sum = 0;
                for (long n; (n = numbers.take()) != END; ) sum += n;
                total.set(sum);
                return null;
            });
        }                                                              // close() waits for all stages
        return total.get();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        int n = 100_000;
        List&lt;String&gt; input = java.util.stream.IntStream.rangeClosed(1, n).mapToObj(i -&gt; " " + i + " ").toList();
        long sum = run(input, 8);
        long expected = (long) n * (n + 1) / 2;
        System.out.printf("pipeline sum=%d expected=%d%n", sum, expected);
        if (sum != expected) throw new AssertionError();
        System.out.println("PASS");
    }
}
</code></pre>

Algorithm Bursts Memory Accuracy Token bucket Allows up to B O(1) Good Leaky bucket Smooths to a constant rate O(1) or a queue Good Fixed window counter 2× burst at window edges O(1) Poor at edges Sliding window log None O(requests) Exact Sliding window counter Small O(1) Approximate, good Distributed rate limiting across 20 pods? <p>A local bucket per pod with <code>R/20</code> is simple but inaccurate when traffic is uneven. For accuracy, use a Redis Lua script that does refill+take atomically per key (one round trip), with fail-open or fail-closed decided per endpoint.</p> Per-user limits? <p>Use <code>ConcurrentHashMap&lt;UserId, TokenBucket&gt;</code> with <code>computeIfAbsent</code>. Evict idle buckets with Caffeine's <code>expireAfterAccess</code>, or the map grows forever.</p> Can you make it lock-free? <p>Yes: pack <code>(tokens, timestamp)</code> into an immutable object in an <code>AtomicReference</code> and CAS-loop the refill+take. It's worth it only under heavy contention.</p> Full source: TokenBucketRateLimiter.java <pre><code>import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

/**
 * Token-bucket rate limiter: capacity B (burst), refill R tokens/second. tryAcquire() never blocks; acquire() waits.
 * Run: java TokenBucketRateLimiter.java
 */
public class TokenBucketRateLimiter {

    private final long capacity;
    private final double refillPerNano;
    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
    private double tokens;                            // guarded by lock
    private long lastRefillNanos;                     // guarded by lock

    public TokenBucketRateLimiter(long capacity, long tokensPerSecond) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.refillPerNano = tokensPerSecond / 1_000_000_000.0;
        this.tokens = capacity;                       // start full → allows an initial burst
        this.lastRefillNanos = System.nanoTime();
    }

    /** Lazy refill: no background thread; tokens are computed from elapsed time on each call. */
    private void refill(long now) {
        tokens = Math.min(capacity, tokens + (now - lastRefillNanos) * refillPerNano);
        lastRefillNanos = now;
    }

    public boolean tryAcquire() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            refill(System.nanoTime());
            if (tokens &gt;= 1) {
                tokens -= 1;
                return true;
            }
            return false;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public void acquire() throws InterruptedException {
        while (true) {
            long waitNanos;
            lock.lock();
            try {
                refill(System.nanoTime());
                if (tokens &gt;= 1) {
                    tokens -= 1;
                    return;
                }
                waitNanos = (long) Math.ceil((1 - tokens) / refillPerNano);   // time until 1 token exists
            } finally {
                lock.unlock();
            }
            Thread.sleep(Duration.ofNanos(waitNanos));   // sleep OUTSIDE the lock; cheap on a virtual thread
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        var limiter = new TokenBucketRateLimiter(10, 100);       // burst 10, 100/s
        AtomicInteger allowed = new AtomicInteger();
        long end = System.nanoTime() + Duration.ofMillis(500).toNanos();
        try (var ex = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
            for (int c = 0; c &lt; 50; c++) {                        // 50 greedy clients
                ex.submit(() -&gt; { while (System.nanoTime() &lt; end) { if (limiter.tryAcquire()) allowed.incrementAndGet(); Thread.onSpinWait(); } });
            }
        }
        // expected ≈ burst 10 + 0.5 s x 100/s = 60
        System.out.println("tryAcquire: 50 greedy clients for 500 ms -&gt; allowed " + allowed.get() + " (expected ~60)");
        if (allowed.get() &lt; 50 || allowed.get() &gt; 70) throw new AssertionError();

        var blocking = new TokenBucketRateLimiter(1, 200);       // no burst, 200/s
        long t0 = System.nanoTime();
        try (var ex = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; 101; i++) ex.submit(() -&gt; { blocking.acquire(); return null; });
        }
        long ms = (System.nanoTime() - t0) / 1_000_000;
        System.out.println("acquire: 101 permits at 200/s took " + ms + " ms (expected ~500)");
        if (ms &lt; 400 || ms &gt; 1_500) throw new AssertionError();
        System.out.println("PASS");
    }
}
