How do you add expiry? <p>Store <code>(future, loadedAt)</code>, and on access treat old entries as missing (<code>remove(key, old)</code> then retry). Or use Caffeine's <code>AsyncLoadingCache</code>, which implements exactly this pattern plus <code>refreshAfterWrite</code> (serving stale data while one thread refreshes).</p> What if a waiter has a shorter deadline than the load? <p>Use <code>existing.get(timeout)</code> or <code>orTimeout</code> on a copy. Never cancel the shared future, because that would fail everyone else too.</p> Where else is this pattern used? <p>Go's <code>singleflight</code> package, CDN request collapsing, DataLoader batching in GraphQL, and JVM class loading (the per-class-name lock).</p> Full source: SingleFlightCache.java <pre><code>import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutionException;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;
import java.util.function.Function;

/**
 * Request coalescing ("single flight"): when 1,000 threads ask for the same missing key at once, the expensive
 * loader runs ONCE and everyone shares the result. Prevents cache stampedes / thundering herds.
 * Run: java SingleFlightCache.java
 */
public class SingleFlightCache&lt;K, V&gt; {

    private final ConcurrentHashMap&lt;K, CompletableFuture&lt;V&gt;&gt; cache = new ConcurrentHashMap&lt;&gt;();
    private final Function&lt;K, V&gt; loader;

    public SingleFlightCache(Function&lt;K, V&gt; loader) { this.loader = loader; }

    public V get(K key) throws InterruptedException, ExecutionException {
        CompletableFuture&lt;V&gt; mine = new CompletableFuture&lt;&gt;();
        CompletableFuture&lt;V&gt; existing = cache.putIfAbsent(key, mine);   // atomic: exactly one thread wins
        if (existing != null) {
            return existing.get();                                       // losers wait for the winner's result
        }
        try {                                                            // winner loads OUTSIDE any map lock
            V value = loader.apply(key);
            mine.complete(value);
            return value;
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            cache.remove(key, mine);                                     // don't cache failures: allow a retry
            mine.completeExceptionally(e);                               // ...but fail everyone currently waiting
            throw e;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        AtomicInteger loads = new AtomicInteger();
        var cache = new SingleFlightCache&lt;String, String&gt;(key -&gt; {
            loads.incrementAndGet();
            try { Thread.sleep(200); } catch (InterruptedException e) { throw new RuntimeException(e); }   // slow DB
            return key.toUpperCase();
        });
        AtomicInteger correct = new AtomicInteger();
        long t0 = System.nanoTime();
        try (var ex = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1_000; i++) {
                String key = "sku-" + (i % 3);                           // 3 distinct keys
                ex.submit(() -&gt; { if (cache.get(key).equals(key.toUpperCase())) correct.incrementAndGet(); return null; });
            }
        }
        long ms = (System.nanoTime() - t0) / 1_000_000;
        System.out.printf("1,000 concurrent gets on 3 keys: loader calls=%d, correct=%d, took %d ms (one 200 ms load per key, in parallel)%n",
                loads.get(), correct.get(), ms);
        if (loads.get() != 3 || correct.get() != 1_000) throw new AssertionError();

        AtomicInteger attempts = new AtomicInteger();
        var flaky = new SingleFlightCache&lt;String, String&gt;(k -&gt; {
            if (attempts.incrementAndGet() == 1) throw new IllegalStateException("db down");
            return "ok";
        });
        try { flaky.get("x"); } catch (IllegalStateException expected) { /* first call fails */ }
        if (!"ok".equals(flaky.get("x"))) throw new AssertionError("failure must not be cached");
        System.out.println("failed load not cached: retry succeeded");
        System.out.println("PASS");
    }
}
</code></pre>

Why not virtual threads here? <p>The work is pure CPU. There are only 4 cores, so 4 carriers. Virtual threads add scheduling overhead and no parallelism, and they aren't preempted, so long CPU tasks would starve other VTs.</p> How would you parallelize the merge itself? <p>Split the larger half at its median, binary-search that value in the other half, and merge the two resulting pairs in parallel. That's O(log² n) span.</p> Parallel streams vs Fork/Join? <p><code>parallelStream()</code> runs on the same common <code>ForkJoinPool</code> with spliterators doing the splitting. Use it for map/filter/reduce. Write a <code>RecursiveTask</code> when the decomposition is custom.</p> Full source: ParallelMergeSort.java <pre><code>import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Random;
import java.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool;
import java.util.concurrent.RecursiveAction;

/**
 * Parallel merge sort with Fork/Join (divide and conquer, work stealing).
 * Run: java ParallelMergeSort.java
 */
public class ParallelMergeSort {

    static final class SortTask extends RecursiveAction {
        private static final int SEQUENTIAL_THRESHOLD = 8_192;    // below this, forking costs more than it saves
        private final int[] a;
        private final int[] tmp;                                  // shared scratch buffer, disjoint ranges per task
        private final int lo, hi;                                 // sorts a[lo, hi)

        SortTask(int[] a, int[] tmp, int lo, int hi) {
            this.a = a; this.tmp = tmp; this.lo = lo; this.hi = hi;
        }

        @Override
        protected void compute() {
            if (hi - lo &lt;= SEQUENTIAL_THRESHOLD) {
                Arrays.sort(a, lo, hi);                           // leaf: sequential sort
                return;
            }
            int mid = (lo + hi) &gt;&gt;&gt; 1;                            // &gt;&gt;&gt; avoids int overflow for huge arrays
            invokeAll(new SortTask(a, tmp, lo, mid),              // fork left, run right, join both
                      new SortTask(a, tmp, mid, hi));
            merge(mid);                                           // both halves are sorted now
        }

        private void merge(int mid) {
            System.arraycopy(a, lo, tmp, lo, hi - lo);
            int i = lo, j = mid, k = lo;
            while (i &lt; mid &amp;&amp; j &lt; hi) a[k++] = tmp[i] &lt;= tmp[j] ? tmp[i++] : tmp[j++];   // &lt;= keeps it stable
            while (i &lt; mid) a[k++] = tmp[i++];
            while (j &lt; hi) a[k++] = tmp[j++];
        }
    }

    static void parallelSort(int[] a) {
        ForkJoinPool.commonPool().invoke(new SortTask(a, new int[a.length], 0, a.length));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] data = new Random(7).ints(20_000_000).toArray();
        int[] seq = data.clone(), par = data.clone();

        long t0 = System.nanoTime();
        sequentialMergeSort(seq, new int[seq.length], 0, seq.length);
        long seqMs = (System.nanoTime() - t0) / 1_000_000;

        t0 = System.nanoTime();
        parallelSort(par);
        long parMs = (System.nanoTime() - t0) / 1_000_000;

        int[] ref = data.clone();
        Arrays.sort(ref);
        if (!Arrays.equals(par, ref) || !Arrays.equals(seq, ref)) throw new AssertionError("not sorted");
        System.out.printf("20M ints on %d cores: sequential merge sort %d ms, fork/join %d ms (%.1fx)%n",
                Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors(), seqMs, parMs, (double) seqMs / parMs);
        System.out.println("PASS");
    }

    static void sequentialMergeSort(int[] a, int[] tmp, int lo, int hi) {
        if (hi - lo &lt;= 8_192) { Arrays.sort(a, lo, hi); return; }
        int mid = (lo + hi) &gt;&gt;&gt; 1;
        sequentialMergeSort(a, tmp, lo, mid);
        sequentialMergeSort(a, tmp, mid, hi);
        new SortTask(a, tmp, lo, hi).merge(mid);
    }
}
</code></pre>

Return early if all vendors answer before the deadline? <p>Both versions already do: <code>allOf</code> and <code>invokeAll</code> complete as soon as every task is done.</p> Return the first quote under ₹40 immediately? <p>Use an <code>ExecutorCompletionService</code> and take results as they complete, stopping at the first one that qualifies, then <code>shutdownNow()</code>. With structured concurrency (Java 25), write a custom <code>Joiner</code> whose <code>onComplete</code> returns <code>true</code> to cancel the scope.</p> And with structured concurrency? <p><code>StructuredTaskScope.open(Joiner.awaitAll(), cf -&gt; cf.withTimeout(deadline))</code>, fork each vendor, <code>join()</code>, then collect the subtasks in state <code>SUCCESS</code>. See Structured Concurrency.</p> Full source: BestPriceFanOut.java <pre><code>import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;
import java.util.concurrent.Callable;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.Future;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

/**
 * Ask N vendors for a quote in parallel; return the cheapest quote that arrives within a deadline, ignoring
 * vendors that fail or are too slow. Solved Java-8 style (CompletableFuture) and Java-21 style (virtual threads).
 * Run: java BestPriceFanOut.java
 */
public class BestPriceFanOut {

    record Quote(String vendor, long priceMinor) {}

    record Vendor(String name, long priceMinor, long latencyMs, boolean fails) {
        Quote quote() throws InterruptedException {
            Thread.sleep(latencyMs);
            if (fails) throw new IllegalStateException(name + " is down");
            return new Quote(name, priceMinor);
        }
    }

    static Optional&lt;Quote&gt; bestJava8(List&lt;Vendor&gt; vendors, Duration deadline, ExecutorService pool) {
        List&lt;CompletableFuture&lt;Optional&lt;Quote&gt;&gt;&gt; futures = vendors.stream()
                .map(v -&gt; CompletableFuture
                        .supplyAsync(() -&gt; {
                            try { return Optional.of(v.quote()); }
                            catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); return Optional.&lt;Quote&gt;empty(); }
                        }, pool)
                        .completeOnTimeout(Optional.empty(), deadline.toMillis(), TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS) // too slow
                        .exceptionally(ex -&gt; Optional.empty()))                                         // failed
                .toList();
        return CompletableFuture.allOf(futures.toArray(CompletableFuture[]::new))
                .thenApply(ignored -&gt; futures.stream()
                        .map(CompletableFuture::join)                 // all done: join() won't block
                        .flatMap(Optional::stream)
                        .min(Comparator.comparingLong(Quote::priceMinor)))
                .join();
        // Note: completeOnTimeout does NOT stop the slow vendor call; its pool thread stays busy.
    }

    static Optional&lt;Quote&gt; bestJava21(List&lt;Vendor&gt; vendors, Duration deadline) throws InterruptedException {
        try (var ex = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
            List&lt;Callable&lt;Quote&gt;&gt; calls = vendors.stream().&lt;Callable&lt;Quote&gt;&gt;map(v -&gt; v::quote).toList();
            List&lt;Future&lt;Quote&gt;&gt; results = ex.invokeAll(calls, deadline.toMillis(), TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
            // invokeAll returns when all finished OR the deadline passed; unfinished ones are CANCELLED (interrupted)
            return results.stream()
                    .filter(f -&gt; f.state() == Future.State.SUCCESS)   // skip FAILED and CANCELLED
                    .map(Future::resultNow)
                    .min(Comparator.comparingLong(Quote::priceMinor));
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        List&lt;Vendor&gt; vendors = List.of(
                new Vendor("fresh-mart", 52_00, 80, false),
                new Vendor("green-grocer", 47_00, 120, false),
                new Vendor("cheap-but-slow", 39_00, 2_000, false),   // cheapest, but misses the deadline
                new Vendor("broken", 10_00, 30, true),               // fails
                new Vendor("daily-needs", 49_00, 60, false));
        Duration deadline = Duration.ofMillis(300);

        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(8);
        long t0 = System.nanoTime();
        Optional&lt;Quote&gt; j8 = bestJava8(vendors, deadline, pool);
        long j8ms = (System.nanoTime() - t0) / 1_000_000;
        pool.shutdownNow();

        t0 = System.nanoTime();
        Optional&lt;Quote&gt; j21 = bestJava21(vendors, deadline);
        long j21ms = (System.nanoTime() - t0) / 1_000_000;

        System.out.printf("Java 8 : %s in %d ms%nJava 21: %s in %d ms%n", j8.orElseThrow(), j8ms, j21.orElseThrow(), j21ms);
        if (!j8.orElseThrow().vendor().equals("green-grocer") || !j21.equals(j8)) throw new AssertionError();
        if (j8ms &gt; 1_000 || j21ms &gt; 1_000) throw new AssertionError("deadline not honoured");
        System.out.println("PASS");
    }
}
